.TH ATLAS 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas \- expanded manuals for the tools used in CyberSci preparation
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each tool has a page named atlas\-TOOL with a purpose line, when to use it, an annotated command, a flag table that says when each flag helps, recipes with real captured output, common mistakes and practice tasks.
.SH USAGE
.nf
man atlas\-grep
gm grep        # atlas page if present, else the normal man page
.fi
.SH PAGES
.TP
\fBatlas\-grep\fR
Search text (or binary data) for lines that match a pattern, and print them.
.TP
\fBatlas\-find\fR
Walk a folder tree and list (or act on) files that match conditions such as name, size, type, time or permissions.
.TP
\fBatlas\-sed\fR
A stream editor: read lines, change them with rules (mostly search\-and\-replace), print the result.
.TP
\fBatlas\-awk\fR
Treat each line as fields and run a tiny program: print columns, filter, count, add up.
.TP
\fBatlas\-file\-xxd\-strings\fR
The first three commands for any unknown file: what is it (\fBfile\fR), what are its bytes (\fBxxd\fR), what text is inside (\fBstrings\fR).
.TP
\fBatlas\-curl\fR
Send exactly the HTTP request you describe and show exactly what comes back.
.TP
\fBatlas\-nc\fR
Open a plain TCP (or UDP) connection and pipe text or files through it: the Swiss army knife for talking to services.
.TP
\fBatlas\-openssl\fR
A crypto toolbox: hashes, encoding, symmetric encryption, certificates and keys, from one command.
.TP
\fBatlas\-python\-oneliners\fR
Use \fBpython3 \-c\fR for the small decode, convert and brute\-force jobs that come up in every CTF.
.TP
\fBatlas\-readelf\-objdump\fR
Read a Linux program without running it: headers, symbols, sections, protections and disassembly.
.TP
\fBatlas\-nmap\fR
Discover hosts, open ports, services and versions on a network, and run scripts against them.
.TP
\fBatlas\-tcpdump\fR
Capture and print network packets from the command line, or save them for Wireshark.
.TP
\fBatlas\-gdb\fR
Run a program under a debugger: stop it, look at registers and memory, step instruction by instruction.
.TP
\fBatlas\-sqlite3\fR
Open a SQLite database file and run SQL on it: list tables, read rows, search, count.
